    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d312">

                <pb facs="0451" n="381" sameAs="frus:frus1862/pg381"/>

                <head>Mr. Seward to Mr. Dayton.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 220.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <hi
                                rendition="#italic"> Washington,</hi></placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1862-09-19"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >September</hi> 19, 1862.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> I have received your interesting despatch
                    of September 3, (No. 189.) A republican education has, indeed, made all of us
                    politicians; but it must now be confessed that the same education has also made
                    us soldiers, as cheerful to fight the battles of our country as we are bold to
                    discuss its affairs. I think no nation has ever exhibited such voluntary
                    armies.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>WILLIAM H. SEWARD.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">William L. Dayton</hi>, Esq., <hi
                            rendition="#italic">&amp;c., &amp;c., &amp;c.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
